  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "At the bar, brokenhearted Mikhail Dzhekovich pours Barinov a drink to toast Eleonora's wedding, before stumbling upon Kristina and Nagiyev arguing fiercely over money in a quiet room.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Витя, садись, выпьем.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Сегодня же такой счастливый день.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Уж я-то знаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "А это кто?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Бывшая жена Нагиева.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Миша, ты куда?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Где мои деньги?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Нет никаких денег!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Вон какой денежный мешок.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "На себя посмотрите, прямо по любви, да?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Да, по любви.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Да, ты прав.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Витя, садись, выпьем.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Vitya, sit down, let's drink.' In Russian, the idea of 'sit down' flows naturally into 'let us drink' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Сегодня же такой счастливый день.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'After all, today is such a happy day.' The grammar packs 'tonight' and 'day' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Уж я-то знаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I certainly know.' The meaning hinges on 'certainly' working together with 'know' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "А это кто?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And who is that?' The grammar packs 'and' and 'who' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Бывшая жена Нагиева.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Nagiyev's ex-wife.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'former' and finishing on 'wife'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Миша, ты куда?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Misha, where are you going?' Notice how Russian expresses 'Misha' and 'where to' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Где мои деньги?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Where is my money?' The meaning hinges on 'where' working together with 'money' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Нет никаких денег!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'There is no money!' Mikhail brings out the contrast between 'no' and 'money' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Вон какой денежный мешок.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Look what a moneybag you found.' Mikhail anchors the line on 'over there', while 'sack' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "На себя посмотрите, прямо по любви, да?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Look at yourselves, right out of love, huh?' In Russian, the idea of 'on' flows naturally into 'yes' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Да, по любви.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, out of love.' The meaning hinges on 'yes' working together with 'love' in spoken Russian. Kristina answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Да, ты прав.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, you are right.' Kristina anchors the line on 'yes', while 'right' completes the thought. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
